{"version":1,"slug":"organized__Others__Quantitative_Aptitude__General_Quant__SSC_CGL_MOCKS_2025_SSC_SUPER_PRACTICE_37249","path":"organized/Others/Quantitative Aptitude/General Quant/@SSC_CGL_MOCKS_2025_SSC SUPER PRACTICE_37249.html","title":"devgagan","provider":"organized","category":"Others/Quantitative Aptitude/General Quant","durationMinutes":60,"totalQuestions":20,"sectionsLocked":false,"sections":null,"questions":[{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>A square of a number added to its 4 times gives 437 as a solution. Find the number</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2325;&#2367;&#2360;&#2368; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2325;&#2375; &#2357;&#2352;&#2381;&#2327; &#2325;&#2379; &#2313;&#2360;&#2325;&#2375; 4 &#2327;&#2369;&#2344;&#2366; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2332;&#2379;&#2337;&#2364;&#2344;&#2375; &#2346;&#2352; &#2361;&#2354; 437 &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2366;&#2346;&#2381;&#2340; &#2361;&#2379;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;&#2404; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2332;&#2381;&#2334;&#2366;&#2340; &#2325;&#2368;&#2332;&#2367;&#2319;</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">23</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">23</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">21</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">21</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">26</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">26</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">19</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">19</span>"],"correct_option_id":3,"solution":"<p>Let the number be x, then according to condition x<sup>2</sup> + 4x = 437, </p> <p>On solving this we get x = 19, -23 From the options the number is 19.</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>The sum of the digits that must be multiplied by 1500 so as to make it a perfect cube is</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2346;&#2370;&#2352;&#2381;&#2339; &#2328;&#2344; &#2348;&#2344;&#2366;&#2344;&#2375; &#2325;&#2375; &#2354;&#2367;&#2319; 1500 &#2360;&#2375; &#2327;&#2369;&#2339;&#2366; &#2325;&#2367;&#2319; &#2332;&#2366;&#2344;&#2375; &#2357;&#2366;&#2354;&#2375; &#2309;&#2306;&#2325;&#2379;&#2306; &#2325;&#2366; &#2351;&#2379;&#2327; &#2325;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">9</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">9</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">5</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">5</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">6</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">6</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">7</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">7</span>"],"correct_option_id":0,"solution":"<p>1500 = 3*5*5*5*2*2</p><p>To make it a perfect cube, it must be multiplied with</p><p>3*2*3 = 18</p><p>Sum of digit = 1+8</p><p>= 9</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>Find the product of all the prime numbers between 70 and 80.</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>70 &#2324;&#2352; 80 &#2325;&#2375; &#2348;&#2368;&#2330; &#2360;&#2349;&#2368; &#2309;&#2349;&#2366;&#2332;&#2381;&#2351; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2323;&#2306; &#2325;&#2366; &#2327;&#2369;&#2339;&#2344;&#2347;&#2354; &#2332;&#2381;&#2334;&#2366;&#2340; &#2325;&#2368;&#2332;&#2367;&#2319;&#2404;</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">518337</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">518337</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">409457</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">409457</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">560946</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">560946</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">576727</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">576727</span>"],"correct_option_id":1,"solution":"<p>Prime numbers are numbers that are only divisible by themselves or 1.</p><p>The prime numbers between 70 and 80 are 71, 73 and 79.</p><p>Therefore their product = 71*73*79</p><p>Product = 409457</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>The sum of two numbers is 80 and their product is 1564. What will be the sum of their reciprocals? </p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2342;&#2379; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2323;&#2306; &#2325;&#2366; &#2351;&#2379;&#2327; 80 &#2361;&#2376; &#2324;&#2352; &#2313;&#2344;&#2325;&#2366; &#2327;&#2369;&#2339;&#2344;&#2347;&#2354; 1564 &#2361;&#2376;&#2404; &#2313;&#2344;&#2325;&#2375; &#2357;&#2381;&#2351;&#2369;&#2340;&#2381;&#2325;&#2381;&#2352;&#2350;&#2379;&#2306; &#2325;&#2366; &#2351;&#2379;&#2327; &#2325;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2361;&#2379;&#2327;&#2366;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">25/391</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">25/391</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">15/391</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">15/391</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">40/391</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">40/391</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">20/391</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">20/391</span>"],"correct_option_id":3,"solution":"<p>Let the number be x and y respectively.</p><p>x + y = 80</p><p>x = 80  -  y</p><p>x * y = 1564</p><p>(80  -  y) * y = 1564</p><p>80y  -  y<sup>2</sup> = 1564</p><p>y<sup>2 </sup> -  80y + 1564 = 0</p><p>y = 34, 46</p><p>The required value = 1/34 + 1/46 = (23 + 17)/782 = 20/391</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>If P and Q are two distinct prime numbers less than 20, then which of the following statement cannot be true?</p><p>I: 2P + 3Q can be even.</p><p>II: Maximum value of (P + Q)/(P  -  Q) is more than 15.</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2351;&#2342;&#2367; P &#2324;&#2352; Q 20 &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2350; &#2342;&#2379; &#2349;&#2367;&#2344;&#2381;&#2344; &#2309;&#2349;&#2366;&#2332;&#2381;&#2351; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2319;&#2305; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306;, &#2340;&#2379; &#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2380;&#2344; &#2360;&#2366; &#2325;&#2341;&#2344; &#2360;&#2340;&#2381;&#2351; &#2344;&#2361;&#2368;&#2306; &#2361;&#2379; &#2360;&#2325;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;?</p><p>I: 2P + 3Q &#2360;&#2350; &#2361;&#2379; &#2360;&#2325;&#2340;&#2375; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306;&#2404;</p><p>II: (P + Q)/(P - Q) &#2325;&#2366; &#2309;&#2343;&#2367;&#2325;&#2340;&#2350; &#2350;&#2366;&#2344; 15 &#2360;&#2375; &#2309;&#2343;&#2367;&#2325; &#2361;&#2376;&#2404;</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">Only I</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; I</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Only II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; II</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Both I and II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">I &#2324;&#2352; II &#2342;&#2379;&#2344;&#2379;&#2306;</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Neither I nor II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2344; &#2340;&#2379; I &#2324;&#2352; &#2344; &#2361;&#2368; II</span>"],"correct_option_id":3,"solution":"<p>P and Q can be 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17 or 19.</p><p>I: If we take Q = 2, then the value of 2P + 3Q will be even. </p><p>II: For maximum value, the value of (P+Q) is maximum and (P - Q) is minimum.</p><p>Let P = 19 and Q = 17</p><p>(17 + 19)/(19  -  17) = 18</p><p>So, option 'd' is correct.</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>If m and n are whole numbers such that m<sup>n</sup> = 121, then the value of (m  -  1)<sup>n + 1</sup> is</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2351;&#2342;&#2367; m &#2324;&#2352; n &#2346;&#2370;&#2352;&#2381;&#2339; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2319;&#2306; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306; &#2332;&#2376;&#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2367; m<sup>n</sup> = 121 &#2361;&#2376;, &#2340;&#2379; (m - 1) n + 1 &#2325;&#2366; &#2350;&#2366;&#2344; &#2361;&#2376;:__</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">1</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">1</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">10</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">10</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">121</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">121</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">1000</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">1000</span>"],"correct_option_id":3,"solution":"<p>We know that,</p><p>11<sup>2</sup> = 121</p><p>Putting m = 11 and n = 2, we get</p><p>(m  -  1)<sup>n + 1</sup> = (11  -  1)<sup>2 + 1</sup> = 10<sup>3</sup> = 1000</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>The sum of square of two positive numbers is 808 and cube of one number is 2904 more than the square of its 4 times, then find the second number?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2342;&#2379; &#2343;&#2344;&#2366;&#2340;&#2381;&#2350;&#2325; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2323;&#2306; &#2325;&#2375; &#2357;&#2352;&#2381;&#2327; &#2325;&#2366; &#2351;&#2379;&#2327; 808 &#2361;&#2376; &#2324;&#2352; &#2319;&#2325; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2325;&#2366; &#2328;&#2344;, &#2311;&#2360;&#2325;&#2375; 4 &#2327;&#2369;&#2344;&#2366; &#2325;&#2375; &#2357;&#2352;&#2381;&#2327; &#2360;&#2375; 2904 &#2309;&#2343;&#2367;&#2325; &#2361;&#2376;, &#2340;&#2379; &#2342;&#2370;&#2360;&#2352;&#2368; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2332;&#2381;&#2334;&#2366;&#2340; &#2325;&#2352;&#2375;&#2306;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">24</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">24</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">18</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">18</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">20</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">20</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">16</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">16</span>"],"correct_option_id":1,"solution":"<p>Let two numbers be A and B.</p><p>A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup> = 808</p><p>And A<sup>3</sup> = 2904 + (4A)<sup>2</sup></p><p>=&gt; A<sup>3</sup>  -  16A<sup>2</sup> = 2904</p><p>=&gt; A<sup>2</sup>(A  -  16) = 484 * 6 = 22<sup>2</sup> * (22  -  16)</p><p>So, A = 22</p><p>So, B<sup>2</sup> = 808  -  22<sup>2</sup> = 324</p><p>=&gt; B = 18</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>There are two numbers X and Y such that X is equal to the sum of all prime numbers between 118 and 140 and Y is equal to the sum of composite numbers from 61 to 97, then what is the difference between X and Y?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2342;&#2379; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2366;&#2319;&#2306; X &#2324;&#2352; Y &#2311;&#2360; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2325;&#2366;&#2352; &#2360;&#2375; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306; &#2325;&#2367; X, 118 &#2324;&#2352; 140 &#2325;&#2375; &#2348;&#2368;&#2330; &#2360;&#2349;&#2368; &#2309;&#2349;&#2366;&#2332;&#2381;&#2351; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2323;&#2306; &#2325;&#2375; &#2351;&#2379;&#2327; &#2325;&#2375; &#2348;&#2352;&#2366;&#2348;&#2352; &#2361;&#2376; &#2324;&#2352; Y, 61 &#2360;&#2375; 97 &#2325;&#2375; &#2348;&#2368;&#2330; &#2360;&#2306;&#2351;&#2369;&#2325;&#2381;&#2340; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2323;&#2306; &#2325;&#2375; &#2351;&#2379;&#2327; &#2325;&#2375; &#2348;&#2352;&#2366;&#2348;&#2352; &#2361;&#2376;, &#2340;&#2379; X &#2324;&#2352; Y &#2325;&#2375; &#2348;&#2368;&#2330; &#2325;&#2366; &#2309;&#2306;&#2340;&#2352; &#2325;&#2367;&#2340;&#2344;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">1689</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">1689</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">1678</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">1678</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">1769</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">1769</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">1789</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">1789</span>"],"correct_option_id":2,"solution":"<p>All prime numbers between 118 and 140 = {127, 131, 137, 139}</p><p>Sum = 127 + 131 + 137 + 139 = 534</p><p>Sum of all natural-numbers from 61 to 97 = (97  -  61 + 1)/2 * (61 + 97) = 2923</p><p>And all prime numbers from 61 to 97 = {61, 67, 71, 73, 79, 83, 89, 97}</p><p>Sum = 61 + 67 + 71 + 73 + 79 + 83 + 89 + 97 = 620</p><p>So, sum of all composite numbers from 61 to 97 = 2923  -  620 = 2303</p><p>So, required difference = 2303  -  534 = 1769</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>abc denotes a 3-digit number. If a and b are interchanged, the value of the original number decreases by 90. How many possible pairs (a, b) exist?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>abc 3- &#2309;&#2306;&#2325;&#2379; &#2325;&#2368; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2325;&#2379; &#2342;&#2352;&#2381;&#2358;&#2366;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376; | &#2351;&#2342;&#2367; a &#2324;&#2352; b &#2325;&#2379; &#2319;&#2325; &#2342;&#2370;&#2360;&#2352;&#2375; &#2360;&#2375; &#2309;&#2342;&#2354;&#2366;-&#2348;&#2342;&#2354;&#2368; &#2325;&#2352; &#2342;&#2368; &#2332;&#2366;&#2319; ,&#2340;&#2379; &#2350;&#2370;&#2354; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2325;&#2366; &#2350;&#2366;&#2344; 90 &#2325;&#2350; &#2361;&#2379; &#2332;&#2366;&#2319;&#2327;&#2366; | (a, b) &#2325;&#2375; &#2325;&#2367;&#2340;&#2344;&#2375; &#2360;&#2306;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;&#2367;&#2340; &#2332;&#2379;&#2396;&#2379; &#2325;&#2368; &#2360;&#2306;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;&#2344;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376; ?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">9</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">9</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">10</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">10</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">15</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">15</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">12</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">12</span>"],"correct_option_id":0,"solution":"<p>100a + 10b + c  -  (100b + 10a + c) = 90 </p> <p>90a  -  90b = 90&#8594; a  -  b = 1...(i). Also, a &gt; 0 and a and b both are less than 10. There are 9 pairs of (a, b) satisfying equation (i). </p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>x, y, and z are distinct prime numbers where x &lt; y &lt; z. If xy + yz = 154, then what is the sum of possible values of z?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>x, y, &#2324;&#2352; z &#2357;&#2367;&#2358;&#2367;&#2359;&#2381;&#2335; &#2309;&#2349;&#2366;&#2332;&#2381;&#2351; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2319;&#2305; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306; &#2332;&#2361;&#2366;&#2305; x &lt; y &lt; z &#2361;&#2376;&#2306;&#2404; &#2351;&#2342;&#2367; xy + yz = 154 &#2361;&#2376;, &#2340;&#2379; z &#2325;&#2375; &#2360;&#2306;&#2349;&#2366;&#2357;&#2367;&#2340; &#2350;&#2366;&#2344;&#2379;&#2306; &#2325;&#2366; &#2351;&#2379;&#2327;&#2347;&#2354; &#2325;&#2367;&#2340;&#2344;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">32</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">32</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">36</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">36</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">34</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">34</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">44</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">44</span>"],"correct_option_id":1,"solution":"<p>xy + yz = 154</p><p>=&gt; y(x + z) = 7 * 22</p><p>Since, y &lt; z. So, y &lt; (x + z)</p><p>Thus, y = 7 and x + z = 22</p><p>Now, putting x = 2, and z = 20 (not possible)</p><p>Again, putting x = 3, and z = 19 (possible)</p><p>Again, putting x = 5, and z = 17 (possible)</p><p>Again, putting x = 7, and z = 15 (not possible)</p><p>Again, putting x = 11, and z = 11 (not possible)</p><p>Hence, sum of possible values of z = 19 + 17 = 36</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>Which of the following can be express as the sum of two prime numbers?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344;&#2354;&#2367;&#2326;&#2367;&#2340; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2367;&#2360;&#2375; &#2342;&#2379; &#2309;&#2349;&#2366;&#2332;&#2381;&#2351; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2323;&#2306; &#2325;&#2375; &#2351;&#2379;&#2327; &#2325;&#2375; &#2352;&#2370;&#2346; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2357;&#2381;&#2351;&#2325;&#2381;&#2340; &#2325;&#2367;&#2351;&#2366; &#2332;&#2366; &#2360;&#2325;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">23</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">23</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">29</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">29</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">33</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">33</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">47</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">47</span>"],"correct_option_id":2,"solution":"<p>Checking through options,</p><p>Option 1: 23</p><p>Let 23 = X + Y, where X and Y are two prime numbers. Since 23 is odd, one of X and Y must be 2. But in such case, the other number will be 21, which is not a prime number. Therefore, 23 cannot be expressed as the sum of two primes.</p><p>Option 2: 29</p><p>Let 29 = X + Y, where X and Y are two prime numbers. Since 29 is odd, one of X and Y must be 2. But in such case, the other number will be 27, which is not a prime number. Therefore, 29 cannot be expressed as the sum of two primes.</p><p>Option 3: 33</p><p>Let 33 = X + Y, where X and Y are two prime numbers. Since 33 is odd, one of X and Y must be 2. And the other number will be 31, which is also a prime number. Therefore, 33 can be expressed as the sum of two primes.</p><p>Option 4: 47</p><p>Let 47 = X + Y, where X and Y are two prime numbers. Since 47 is odd, one of X and Y must be 2. But in such case, the other number will be 45, which is not a prime number. Therefore, 47 cannot be expressed as the sum of two primes.</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>Which of the following statement is true?</p><p>I. 226<sup>51</sup> is a prime number.</p><p>II. 316<sup>64</sup> is not a prime number.</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2380;&#2344;-&#2360;&#2366; &#2360;&#2340;&#2381;&#2351; &#2361;&#2376;?</p><p>I. 226<sup>51</sup> &#2319;&#2325; &#2309;&#2349;&#2366;&#2332;&#2381;&#2351; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;|</p><p>II. 316<sup>64</sup> &#2319;&#2325; &#2309;&#2349;&#2366;&#2332;&#2381;&#2351; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2344;&#2361;&#2368;&#2306; &#2361;&#2376;|</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">Only I</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; I</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Only II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; II</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Both I and II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">I &#2324;&#2352; II &#2342;&#2379;&#2344;&#2379;&#2306;</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Neither I nor II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2344; &#2340;&#2379; I &#2324;&#2352; &#2344; &#2361;&#2368; II</span>"],"correct_option_id":1,"solution":"<p>I. 226 is an even number and when an even number is multiplied by itself multiple times, the solution is not a prime number. So, 226<sup>51</sup> is not a prime number.</p><p>Hence, statement I is false.</p><p>II. 316 is an even number and when an even number is multiplied by itself multiple times, the solution is not a prime number. So, 316<sup>64</sup> is not a prime number.</p><p>Hence, statement II is true.</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>Which of the following statement is true?</p><p>I. 16<sup>7</sup>+11<sup>6</sup> is odd</p><p>II. 15<sup>5</sup>-12<sup>4</sup> is even</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344;&#2354;&#2367;&#2326;&#2367;&#2340; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2380;&#2344;-&#2360;&#2366; &#2325;&#2341;&#2344; &#2360;&#2340;&#2381;&#2351; &#2361;&#2376;?</p><p>I. 16<sup>7</sup>+11<sup>6</sup> &#2357;&#2367;&#2359;&#2350; &#2361;&#2376;|</p><p>II. 15<sup>5</sup>-12<sup>4</sup> &#2360;&#2350; &#2361;&#2376;|</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">Only I</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; I</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Only II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; II</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Both I and II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">I &#2324;&#2352; II &#2342;&#2379;&#2344;&#2379;&#2306;</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Neither I nor II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2344; &#2340;&#2379; I &#2324;&#2352; &#2344; &#2361;&#2368; II</span>"],"correct_option_id":0,"solution":"<p>I. 16<sup>7</sup>+11<sup>6</sup></p><p>When an even number is multiplied by another even number then their solution will be even. So,</p><p>16<sup>7</sup>will be even.</p><p>Similarly, when an odd number is multiplied by another odd number then their solution will be odd. So, 11<sup>6</sup> will be odd.</p><p>Sum of an even number and an odd number always gives an odd value.</p><p>So, 16<sup>7</sup>+11<sup>6</sup> is odd.</p><p>Hence, statement I is true.</p><p>II. 15<sup>5</sup>-12<sup>4</sup></p><p>When an odd number is multiplied by another odd number then their solution will be odd. So,</p><p>15<sup>5</sup> will be odd.</p><p>Similarly, when an even number is multiplied by another even number then their solution will be even. So, 12<sup>4</sup>will be even.</p><p>Difference between an even number and an odd number always gives an odd value.</p><p>So, 15<sup>5</sup>-12<sup>4</sup> is odd.</p><p>Hence, statement II is wrong.</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>How many balls Aman has if Ana has 436 balls, which is 112 more than thrice the number of balls Aman has? </p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2351;&#2342;&#2367; &#2319;&#2344;&#2366; &#2325;&#2375; &#2346;&#2366;&#2360; 436 &#2327;&#2375;&#2306;&#2342;&#2375;&#2306; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306;, &#2332;&#2379; &#2309;&#2350;&#2344; &#2325;&#2368; &#2327;&#2375;&#2306;&#2342;&#2379;&#2306; &#2325;&#2368; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2325;&#2375; &#2340;&#2368;&#2344; &#2327;&#2369;&#2344;&#2375; &#2360;&#2375; 112 &#2309;&#2343;&#2367;&#2325; &#2361;&#2376;, &#2340;&#2379; &#2309;&#2350;&#2344; &#2325;&#2375; &#2346;&#2366;&#2360; &#2325;&#2367;&#2340;&#2344;&#2368; &#2327;&#2375;&#2306;&#2342;&#2375;&#2306; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306; ?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">108</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">108</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">103</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">103</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">141</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">141</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">124</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">124</span>"],"correct_option_id":0,"solution":"<p>It is given that,</p><p>Ana has 436 balls.</p><p>436 = 112 + 3 x Aman</p><p>436 - 112 = 3 x Aman</p><p>324 = 3 x Aman</p><p>324/3 = Aman</p><p>Aman = 108. </p><p>Hence, Aman has <b>108</b> balls. </p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>If A = 4&radic;7, B = 5&radic;5 and C = 3&radic;11, then which of the following statement(s) is/are true?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2351;&#2342;&#2367; A = 4&radic;7, B = 5&radic;5 &#2324;&#2352; C = 3&radic;11 &#2361;&#2376;, &#2340;&#2379; &#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344;&#2354;&#2367;&#2326;&#2367;&#2340; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2380;&#2344; &#2360;&#2366; &#2325;&#2341;&#2344; &#2360;&#2340;&#2381;&#2351; &#2361;&#2376;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">A &gt; B &lt; C</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">A &gt; B &lt; C</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">A &gt; C &gt; B</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">A &gt; C &gt; B</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">B &lt; C &gt; A</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">B &lt; C &gt; A</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">B &gt; A &gt; C</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">B &gt; A &gt; C</span>"],"correct_option_id":3,"solution":"<p>A = 4&radic;7, B = 5&radic;5 and C = 3&radic;11</p><p>Taking square of A, B and C we get,</p><p>A<sup>2</sup> = 112, B<sup>2</sup> = 125 and C<sup>2</sup> = 99</p><p>Then, A<sup>2</sup> &lt; B<sup>2</sup>,<sup>, </sup>B<sup>2</sup> &gt; C<sup>2</sup> and A<sup>2</sup> &gt; C<sup>2</sup></p><p>Therefore, A &lt; B, B &gt; C, A &gt; C</p><p>Hence, option (d) is the correct answer.</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>How many whole numbers lie between 24<sup>2</sup> and 25<sup>2</sup>?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>24<sup>2</sup> &#2324;&#2352; 25<sup>2</sup> &#2325;&#2375; &#2348;&#2368;&#2330; &#2325;&#2367;&#2340;&#2344;&#2368; &#2346;&#2370;&#2352;&#2381;&#2339; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2319;&#2305; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">48</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">48</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">35</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">35</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">49</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">49</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">36</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">36</span>"],"correct_option_id":0,"solution":"<p>Since, the numbers lie between n<sup>2</sup> and (n + 1)<sup>2</sup> = 2n</p><p>So, whole numbers lie between 24<sup>2</sup> and 25<sup>2</sup> = 2*24 = 48</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>How many natural numbers less than 1000 are both perfect squares and perfect cubes?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>1000 &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2350; &#2325;&#2367;&#2340;&#2344;&#2368; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2366;&#2325;&#2371;&#2340; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2319;&#2305; &#2346;&#2370;&#2352;&#2381;&#2339; &#2357;&#2352;&#2381;&#2327; &#2324;&#2352; &#2346;&#2370;&#2352;&#2381;&#2339; &#2328;&#2344; &#2342;&#2379;&#2344;&#2379;&#2306; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">4</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">4</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">5</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">5</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">3</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">3</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">2</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">2</span>"],"correct_option_id":2,"solution":"<p>There are only 3 number less than 1000 which are both perfect squares and perfect cubes = 1, 64, 729</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>Which of the following given value is less than &#8731;14?</p><p>I: 200<sup>1/6</sup></p><p>II: 38420<sup>1/12</sup></p><p>III: 2745<sup>1/9</sup></p><p>IV: 80<sup>1/5</sup></p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344;&#2354;&#2367;&#2326;&#2367;&#2340; &#2342;&#2367;&#2319; &#2327;&#2319; &#2350;&#2366;&#2344;&#2379;&#2306; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2380;&#2344;-&#2360;&#2366; &#8731;14 &#2360;&#2375; &#2325;&#2350; &#2361;&#2376;?</p><p>I: 200<sup>1/6</sup></p><p>II: 38420<sup>1/12</sup></p><p>III: 2745<sup>1/9</sup></p><p>IV: 80<sup>1/5</sup></p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">Only I</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; I</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Only II</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; II</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Only III</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; III</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">Only IV</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2375;&#2357;&#2354; IV</span>"],"correct_option_id":3,"solution":"<p>I: <img width=40 height=21 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image009.png\"></p><p><img width=81 height=21 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image013.png\"></p><p>So, <img width=91 height=21 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image014.png\"></p><p>II: <img width=60 height=21 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image010.png\"></p><p><img width=155 height=23 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image015.png\"></p><p>So, <img width=132 height=21 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image016.png\"></p><p>III: <img width=48 height=21 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image011.png\"></p><p><img width=139 height=23 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image017.png\"></p><p>So, <img width=108 height=21 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image018.png\"></p><p>IV: <img width=164 height=23 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image019.png\"></p><p><img width=161 height=23 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image020.png\"></p><p>So, <img width=32 height=21 src=\"https://u1.oliveboard.in/exams/solution/oliveimg/p19497/image012.png\"> < &#8731;14</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>Three positive numbers A, B, and C are co-prime to each other. If product of A and B is 399 and product of B and C is 840 then, the value of A + B  -  C is:</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2340;&#2368;&#2344; &#2343;&#2344;&#2366;&#2340;&#2381;&#2350;&#2325; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2319;&#2305; A, B &#2324;&#2352; C &#2319;&#2325;-&#2342;&#2370;&#2360;&#2352;&#2375; &#2325;&#2368; &#2360;&#2361;-&#2309;&#2349;&#2366;&#2332;&#2381;&#2351; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366;&#2319;&#2305; &#2361;&#2376;&#2306;&#2404; &#2351;&#2342;&#2367; A &#2324;&#2352; B &#2325;&#2366; &#2327;&#2369;&#2339;&#2344;&#2347;&#2354; 399 &#2361;&#2376; &#2340;&#2341;&#2366; B &#2324;&#2352; C &#2325;&#2366; &#2327;&#2369;&#2339;&#2344;&#2347;&#2354; 840 &#2361;&#2376;, &#2340;&#2379; A + B - C &#2325;&#2366; &#2350;&#2366;&#2344; &#2325;&#2367;&#2340;&#2344;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376; ?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">0</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">0</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">3</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">3</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">2</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">2</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">1</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">1</span>"],"correct_option_id":0,"solution":"<p>A * B = 399</p><p>B * C = 840</p><p>(A * B)/ (B * C) = 399/840</p><p>A/C = 19/40</p><p>Since A, B, and C are co  -  prime so, A = 19 and C = 40</p><p>And B = 21</p><p>Now, value of A + B  -  C = 19 + 21  -  40 = 40  -  40 = 0</p>","marks":2,"section":null},{"question":"<span class=\"eqt\"><p>How many times digit '6' appears in numbers which are perfect square from 2 to 300?</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>2 &#2360;&#2375; 300 &#2340;&#2325; &#2346;&#2370;&#2352;&#2381;&#2339; &#2357;&#2352;&#2381;&#2327; &#2325;&#2368; &#2360;&#2306;&#2326;&#2381;&#2351;&#2366; &#2350;&#2375;&#2306; '6' &#2325;&#2367;&#2340;&#2344;&#2368; &#2348;&#2366;&#2352; &#2342;&#2367;&#2326;&#2366;&#2312; &#2342;&#2375;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;?</p></span>","options":["<span class=\"eqt\">10</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">10</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">9</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">9</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">7</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">7</span>","<span class=\"eqt\">6</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">6</span>"],"correct_option_id":3,"solution":"<p>Perfect squares from 2 to 300 in which 6 appears are:</p><p>16, 36, 64, 169, 196, 256</p><p>Hence, 6 appears 6 times in perfect squares from 2 to 300.</p>","marks":2,"section":null}]}